.Dd September 13, 2026
.Dt SQUEUE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm squeue_setup ,
.Nm squeue_enter ,
.Nm squeue_register
.Nd shared-queue asynchronous I/O
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/io_uring.h
.Ft int
.Fn squeue_setup "unsigned entries" "struct io_uring_params *params"
.Ft int
.Fo squeue_enter
.Fa "int fd" "unsigned to_submit" "unsigned min_complete"
.Fa "unsigned flags" "const void *arg" "size_t argsz"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn squeue_register "int fd" "unsigned op" "void *arg" "unsigned nr_args"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm squeue
.Pq "shared queue"
is the native
.Fx
completion-based asynchronous I/O interface.
An application submits I/O operations and reaps their results through a pair of
ring buffers shared between the kernel and the process, so that many operations
can be submitted with a single system call and their completions collected with
none.
It is the native analogue of the Linux
.Em io_uring
interface and shares the same on-ring wire format; the Linux
.Em io_uring
compatibility layer and these native system calls are two front ends over one
kernel engine, in the same relationship that
.Xr kqueue 2
bears to Linux
.Em epoll .
.Pp
.Fn squeue_setup
creates a ring able to hold at least
.Fa entries
submission entries and returns a file descriptor referring to it.
.Fa params
is both an input and an output argument: on input its
.Fa flags
select ring options; on output the kernel fills in the sizes of the
submission and completion queues
.Pq Fa sq_entries , cq_entries ,
the set of supported features
.Pq Fa features ,
and the byte offsets
.Pq Fa sq_off , cq_off
of each ring field.
Three regions are then mapped into the process with
.Xr mmap 2
on the returned descriptor at the offsets
.Dv IORING_OFF_SQ_RING ,
.Dv IORING_OFF_CQ_RING ,
and
.Dv IORING_OFF_SQES .
On this implementation the submission and completion rings share a single
mapping
.Pq Dv IORING_FEAT_SINGLE_MMAP .
.Pp
To submit work the application writes one or more
.Vt struct io_uring_sqe
submission entries into the SQE array, publishes their indices through the
submission ring's tail, and calls
.Fn squeue_enter .
Each completed operation yields a
.Vt struct io_uring_cqe
completion entry in the completion ring carrying the submission's
.Fa user_data
and a result
.Fa res
.Pq a non-negative byte count or other value on success, or a negative errno .
.Fa to_submit
is the number of new submissions to consume;
.Fa min_complete ,
together with
.Dv IORING_ENTER_GETEVENTS
in
.Fa flags ,
is the number of completions to wait for before returning.
Completions may be read directly from the shared ring without any further
system call.
.Fa arg
and
.Fa argsz
are reserved and must be
.Dv NULL
and 0.
.Pp
.Fn squeue_register
associates auxiliary resources with a ring to make them cheaper or safer to
use across many operations.
.Fa op
selects the operation and
.Fa arg / Fa nr_args
describe its argument.
Supported operations include
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_BUFFERS
and
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_BUFFERS
.Pq fixed I/O buffers for READ_FIXED / WRITE_FIXED ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES ,
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_FILES ,
and
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES_UPDATE
.Pq fixed files, referenced from a submission by index with IOSQE_FIXED_FILE ,
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_EVENTFD
and
.Dv IORING_UNREGISTER_EVENTFD
.Pq signal an Xr eventfd 2 on every completion ,
and
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_PROBE
.Pq query which operations the kernel supports .
A fixed file is remembered with the capsicum rights the descriptor held at the
time it was registered, and an operation performed through it can never exceed
those rights.
.Ss Event-loop integration
A ring descriptor is a first-class event source.
It may be passed to
.Xr poll 2
or registered with
.Xr kqueue 2
using
.Dv EVFILT_READ ,
becoming ready whenever unreaped completions are present, or an
.Xr eventfd 2
may be registered with
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_EVENTFD
to be signalled on each completion.
Any of the three lets a ring be multiplexed alongside other descriptors in an
existing event loop.
.Ss Capability mode
The
.Nm
system calls are permitted in capability mode
.Pq Xr cap_enter 2 .
Every operation is subject to the capsicum rights of the descriptor it acts on.
In addition, a ring created by a process in capability mode may operate only on
.Em fixed
files registered with
.Dv IORING_REGISTER_FILES ;
an operation naming a raw descriptor, or one that resolves a name in the
ambient file-system namespace, fails with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE .
A ring is thus a closed, rights-limited set of capabilities that a sandboxed
process may drive asynchronously.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn squeue_setup
returns a new file descriptor on success.
.Fn squeue_enter
returns the number of submission entries consumed.
.Fn squeue_register
returns 0 on success.
.Pp
On failure each returns \-1 and sets
.Va errno .
Note that a
.Em completion
error is delivered as a negative errno in the completion entry's
.Fa res
field and does not cause the submitting call to fail.
.Sh ERRORS
These system calls may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa entries
is zero or too large, an unsupported
.Fa flags
bit or register
.Fa op
was given, or a reserved argument was non-zero.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa params
or a submission argument points outside the process's address space.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not a valid descriptor.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa fd
does not refer to a
.Nm
ring.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
the ring's wired memory would exceed
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
or the system-wide limit
.Pq Va kern.squeue.max_wired_pages .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
a resource of the requested kind is already registered.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
in capability mode, an operation named a descriptor or namespace outside the
ring's registered, rights-limited set.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aio_read 2 ,
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
interface first appeared in 5BSD.
Its on-ring format is compatible with the Linux
.Em io_uring
interface, which the 5BSD Linux compatibility layer exposes through the
.Fn io_uring_setup ,
.Fn io_uring_enter ,
and
.Fn io_uring_register
system calls over the same kernel engine.
